  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "NASA's Mars Climate Orbiter was launched on 11 December 1998 and lost on arrival at Mars on 23 September 1999.",
      "sources": [
        "S1",
        "S2",
        "S3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "Contact was lost when the orbiter passed behind Mars during orbit insertion and was never re-established; NASA concluded it was destroyed or lost because its trajectory took it far too low.",
      "sources": [
        "S1",
        "S2",
        "S5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "During cruise, small thruster firings (angular momentum desaturation) kept the reaction wheels in range; each firing slightly changed the trajectory, and the impulse data was supplied in a ground file to the navigation team for trajectory modelling.",
      "sources": [
        "S4",
        "S5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "The spacecraft team (Lockheed Martin Astronautics, Colorado) supplied thruster impulse data in English units (pound-force seconds); the navigation team (JPL, California) used it as if it were metric (newton-seconds), as the interface specification required.",
      "sources": [
        "S2",
        "S4",
        "S5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "1 pound-force = 4.448 newtons, so values in pound-force seconds read as newton-seconds understate each impulse by a factor of about 4.45 (counted at ~22% of true size).",
      "sources": [
        "S6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "The error accumulated over the roughly nine-month cruise, and desaturation events occurred 10 to 14 times more often than the navigation team expected, because of the asymmetric solar array.",
      "sources": [
        "S5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C7",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "The orbiter was intended to pass about 140–150 km above Mars at orbit insertion but instead came in at about 57 km, too low to survive.",
      "sources": [
        "S5",
        "S1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C8",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "Ed Weiler, NASA Associate Administrator for Space Science, said on 30 September 1999: 'The problem here was not the error, it was the failure of NASA's systems engineering, and the checks and balances in our processes to detect the error.' Narration paraphrases this; the caption quotes it verbatim.",
      "sources": [
        "S2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C9",
      "type": "illustrative",
      "text": "Push sizes shown on screen (e.g. '10 lbf·s') are illustrative examples, not values from the actual mission file.",
      "sources": []
    }
  ],
